18. How would you troubleshoot a production application running on AWS?

   Production Troubleshooting
      → 1. Understand impact + reproduce/identify affected flow
      → 2. Check metrics/alerts → CPU, memory, latency, error rate, traffic
      → 3. Check infrastructure → Pods, nodes, restarts, health checks
      → 4. Check logs → Error/exception logs → Identify affected service
      → 5. Trace request → Distributed tracing across microservices
      → 6. Check dependencies → DB, queues, APIs, network
      → 7. Check recent changes → Deployment/config/infrastructure changes
      → 8. Identify root cause → Fix → Monitor → Prevent recurrence
   -------------------------------------------
   If a customer reports an issue in production, I would follow a systematic
      troubleshooting approach rather than immediately changing anything.

      First, I would understand the impact and identify which functionality or
      users are affected. I would check monitoring dashboards and alerts for
      metrics such as request rate, error rate, latency, CPU, and memory.

      If the application is running on Kubernetes/EKS, I would check the health
      of the Pods and nodes, including Pod restarts, crashes, failed health checks,
      CPU throttling, and memory exhaustion.

      If the infrastructure looks healthy, I would check application logs,
      especially ERROR logs and exceptions, to identify which service is failing.
      Then I would trace the affected request across the microservices using
      distributed tracing or correlation/request IDs.

      I would also check dependencies such as databases, external APIs,
      message queues, network connectivity, and other downstream services.
      I would check whether there were any recent deployments, configuration
      changes, infrastructure changes, or database changes that could have
      introduced the issue.

      Once I identify the root cause, I would apply the appropriate fix or
      rollback, verify the application through metrics and logs, and then add
      monitoring, alerts, tests, or other preventive measures if necessary.

      Overall:

      Customer Issue
         ↓
      Impact / Scope
         ↓
      Metrics & Alerts
         ↓
      Infrastructure / Pods
         ↓
      Application Logs
         ↓
      Distributed Trace
         ↓
      Dependencies + Recent Changes
         ↓
      Root Cause
         ↓
      Fix / Rollback
         ↓
      Verify + Prevent Recurrence

